Does the player start in a "box"? We don't draw grid boxes; could draw painted grid slots behind the line. Add white L-shaped grid boxes at each gap position, projected. That would sell the start. Implement in drawStartArea for each grid slot? We know gaps [16..72] at lateral ±.28. Draw small white brackets at those distances, using points for curve (at start straight). During the race, the grid boxes remain painted on the track near the line (ahead 88). That's realistic. We can draw 7 brackets plus player's at 0? Player's box at distance 0 (behind the camera) not visible. As laps go by, the boxes near the line appear. Draw for gaps 0,16,25,34,43,52,62,72 with lateral ±.28 (player slot maybe .28 at 0? But start grid boxes should be at distance relative to GRID? The grid is before the line by 16-72m; the boxes are at absolute distance 0..72 (i.e., line - 88 ... -16). Draw at d = GRID - gap? Hmm `drawStartArea` computes ahead = GRID - distance. To draw a grid box at absolute distance g (from start, g<GRID), it is at ahead = GRID - g from the player? In world terms: the box is ahead by (GRID - g) - distance. At the start distance=0, box at g=72 is 16m ahead; g=0 is 88m ahead. Wait the rivals are at 16..72m absolute; the line is at 88; so P1 (gap72) is 16m behind the line; P7 (gap16) is 72m behind. The player's box at 0 is 88m behind the line. Hmm then all grid boxes are between 16 and 88m ahead of the player at the start, which matches the cars. As the player is at g=0, their own box is under the camera. Good.

